<h2 id="examples-stacks-section-stack-in-groupoids-of-finite-type-spaces" class="tex-section"><span data-tag="04UH">95.12</span> The stack in groupoids of finite type algebraic spaces</h2>

<p>
Let $p : \mathcal{S}\! \mathit{paces}_{ft} \to (\mathit{Sch}/S)_{fppf}$ be the stack introduced in Section <a href="/tag/04UC" data-tag="04UC">95.8</a> (using the abuse of notation introduced there). We can turn this into a stack in groupoids $p' : \mathcal{S}\! \mathit{paces}_{ft}' \to (\mathit{Sch}/S)_{fppf}$ by the procedure of Categories, Lemma <a href="/tag/03WQ" data-tag="03WQ">4.35.3</a>, see Stacks, Lemma <a href="/tag/03YI" data-tag="03YI">8.5.3</a>. In this particular case this simply means $\mathcal{S}\! \mathit{paces}_{ft}'$ has the same objects as $\mathcal{S}\! \mathit{paces}_{ft}$, i.e., finite type morphisms $X \to U$ where $X$ is an algebraic space over $S$ and $U$ is a scheme over $S$. But the morphisms $(f, g) : X/U \to Y/V$ are now commutative diagrams 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix{ X \ar[d] \ar[r]_ f &  Y \ar[d] \\  U \ar[r]^ g &  V }  \]
</div>
<p>
 which are cartesian. 
</p>
